From a43419e12616b3c79331d2c1e0ef7c08ed01694d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 22:29:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer, windowing/gbm: one change of the display when a
 video starts or stops

Every commit that changes the mode, the HDR signalling or the depth of the HDMI link makes the TV
set itself up again. With the refresh rate adjusted to the video, Kodi set the mode of the video
first (VideoPlayer, before the decoder is open), and the output of the video came with its first
picture: the HDR signalling of an HDR10 or HLG video (a modeset on amdgpu and i915), or the
transport of native Dolby Vision (a modeset with every backend). Two changes of the display, a
second or less apart. When the video stopped, the output lost its HDR signalling or the transport
first, and Kodi set the mode of the GUI after it; only native Dolby Vision on nvidia-drm left in
one commit.

CModeChangeHold:
- START: the change of mode that VideoPlayer makes for a video whose output will be HDR or native
  Dolby Vision (DVBRIDGE::ModeChangeForVideo()) is chosen, and the other subsystems told, but not
  committed. It goes out with the first commit after the renderer of the video is configured
  (CRenderManager::Configure() calls VideoOutputConfigured()), which carries the HDR signalling,
  or with the first frame of native output, which carries the transport. Meanwhile nothing is
  committed, and the display keeps the picture it has. An SDR video is not held: its mode is its
  only change.
- STOP: the commit that takes the HDR signalling of a video away waits for the change to the mode
  of the GUI that follows, and goes out with it.
- The end of native Dolby Vision joins the next mode with every backend (DVModeChangeJoins(), no
  longer for nvidia-drm only): amdgpu and i915 made a full modeset for the end of the tunnel in the
  mode of the video, and another one for the mode of the GUI.

Only with atomic modesetting, the output on, a mode of the same window size, no 3D output, and a
mode that is not the one on the screen. Each hold has a deadline: 3 s for the output of a video
(plus the delay after a change of mode that the user set), 2 s for the change of mode after a
video. After it, what was held goes out alone, as before. A change of mode that comes meanwhile
ends a hold (one for a video that never set up its output gives the mode on the screen back to
the selection), as do blanking the output and a window that goes. Native output that starts over
a STOP hold puts back, when it ends, what Kodi has asked for since (the properties that it keeps
are those on the screen), and no START hold follows a STOP one in the same change for the same
reason.

A host model of the commits of each transition, driven by this class, counts the changes of the
display in 360 transitions (nvidia-drm, amdgpu, i915; SDR, HDR10, HLG, native Dolby Vision; the
GUI at 60, 100, 119.88, 120 and 144 Hz; videos at 23.976 to 59.94 Hz): 210 go from two to one,
none the other way, and a renderer configured after the deadline, or no change of mode after a
stop, gives what it gave before. Twelve mutations of the decisions make the test fail.
